::ENG2_U06_L03_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which perspective is clearest for an oral presentation?{
~A. Libraries have books and school has snacks.#Incorrect. This mixes unrelated ideas.
=B. A supervised after-school study space would help students remain safe and productive while waiting on campus.#Correct. This is clear and focused.
~C. I like some school things.#Incorrect. This is vague and personal.
~D. Hallways, books, snacks, buses.#Incorrect. This is a list, not a perspective.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] What does coherent focus mean?{
~A. Including every idea the speaker knows.#Incorrect. Too many unrelated ideas weaken focus.
~B. Avoiding a main point.#Incorrect. A focused presentation needs a main point.
~C. Speaking without planning.#Incorrect. Planning supports coherence.
=D. Keeping all parts of the message connected to one main point.#Correct. Coherence means the details work together.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.07] Which choice best connects to author purpose?{
=A. A speaker chooses details based on what the speaker wants the audience to understand or do.#Correct. Purpose guides choices.
~B. A speaker's purpose never affects word choice.#Incorrect. Purpose affects message choices.
~C. Purpose only matters in written essays.#Incorrect. Purpose matters in oral communication too.
~D. Purpose means the color of a slide.#Incorrect. Purpose is the reason for communication.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Which detail best supports the argument for after-school study space?{
~A. Some students like mystery novels.#Incorrect. This is not central to the argument.
~B. Cafeteria snacks can be popular.#Incorrect. This distracts from the argument.
=C. Students waiting after school often need a quiet supervised place to complete assignments.#Correct. This directly supports the argument.
~D. The library has shelves.#Incorrect. This is too general.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which phrase is most academic?{
=A. coherent focus#Correct. This is precise lesson vocabulary.
~B. random stuff#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
~C. things I like#Incorrect. This is vague and personal.
~D. kind of whatever#Incorrect. This is informal.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which detail should be removed from a presentation about safe study space?{
~A. Students wait in hallways after dismissal.#Incorrect. This supports the focus.
~B. Students need a quiet place for assignments.#Incorrect. This supports the focus.
~C. Supervision can improve safety.#Incorrect. This supports the focus.
=D. Some students prefer mystery novels.#Correct. This detail distracts from the safe study space focus.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which opening best establishes focus?{
~A. I will talk about many random school topics.#Incorrect. This is unfocused.
=B. Today I will explain why a supervised after-school study space would support students who wait on campus.#Correct. This clearly states the focus.
~C. Here are some things I thought of.#Incorrect. This does not establish focus.
~D. Books and buses and snacks are all around.#Incorrect. This is confusing.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Which statement is a valid focused claim?{
~A. The school is a building.#Incorrect. This is a fact, not a useful claim.
~B. Some people like many things.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=C. The school should provide a supervised study space for students who remain after dismissal.#Correct. This is a focused claim.
~D. Snacks are interesting.#Incorrect. This does not fit the focus.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.R.07] A speaker wants school leaders to act. Which purpose is most accurate?{
~A. To entertain classmates with jokes.#Incorrect. That does not match the goal.
~B. To list unrelated school facts.#Incorrect. That is not a clear purpose.
~C. To avoid explaining the issue.#Incorrect. The speaker must explain the issue.
=D. To persuade school leaders to consider a supervised study space.#Correct. The speaker wants the audience to act.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which word best replaces "thing" in an academic speaking note?{
=A. proposal#Correct. Proposal is precise academic vocabulary in this context.
~B. stuff#Incorrect. This is also vague.
~C. whatever#Incorrect. This is informal.
~D. thingy#Incorrect. This is informal.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which sentence keeps focus best?{
~A. The library should stay open because I like books and lunch is good.#Incorrect. This mixes unrelated ideas.
~B. Buses are late, books exist, and snacks matter.#Incorrect. This is unfocused.
=C. Extended library hours would give students who wait after school a quiet place to complete assignments.#Correct. This stays focused.
~D. Many topics connect to school in some way.#Incorrect. This is too broad.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Which detail weakens the argument because it is least relevant?{
~A. Students need supervision after dismissal.#Incorrect. This is relevant.
=B. Some library chairs are blue.#Correct. This detail does not support the argument.
~C. Students wait in noisy areas.#Incorrect. This is relevant.
~D. Students need time to complete assignments.#Incorrect. This is relevant.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] What should a speaker do after stating a focused perspective?{
=A. Choose details that directly support that perspective.#Correct. Supporting details build coherence.
~B. Add unrelated personal stories.#Incorrect. That weakens coherence.
~C. Change the topic immediately.#Incorrect. That breaks focus.
~D. Avoid evidence and reasoning.#Incorrect. Evidence and reasoning support the perspective.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.R.07] How can purpose help a speaker revise notes?{
~A. Purpose tells the speaker to add every possible detail.#Incorrect. Purpose helps select relevant details.
~B. Purpose is unrelated to revision.#Incorrect. Purpose is central to revision.
~C. Purpose means the notes must be longer.#Incorrect. Length is not the goal.
=D. Purpose helps the speaker decide which details belong and which distract.#Correct. Purpose guides revision.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which phrase best fits a formal presentation?{
~A. a bunch of stuff#Incorrect. This is informal.
~B. cool school things#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
=C. relevant supporting details#Correct. This is academic and precise.
~D. random ideas#Incorrect. This is not formal or focused.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which closing reinforces coherent focus?{
~A. Anyway, those are some thoughts.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. A supervised study space would help students wait safely and use after-school time productively.#Correct. This reinforces the focused perspective.
~C. I also like snacks.#Incorrect. This distracts from the focus.
~D. That is all about many things.#Incorrect. This is unfocused.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Which sentence explains why a detail supports the claim?{
~A. Hallway noise exists.#Incorrect. This does not explain the connection.
~B. The hallway is near classrooms.#Incorrect. This may be true but does not explain the claim.
=C. Hallway noise matters because it makes it harder for waiting students to complete assignments.#Correct. This explains the connection.
~D. Noise is noise.#Incorrect. This is circular.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which set of details is most coherent?{
=A. late buses, hallway noise, homework completion#Correct. These details connect to the need for study space.
~B. library books, snack choices, favorite colors#Incorrect. These do not support one clear point.
~C. sports schedules, lunch menus, posters#Incorrect. These are unrelated to the focus.
~D. classroom paint, book genres, weekend plans#Incorrect. These distract from the focus.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.R.07] Which speaking choice shows awareness of purpose?{
~A. Add jokes instead of explaining the need.#Incorrect. This does not serve the purpose.
=B. Explain how the study space would solve a student need.#Correct. This fits the purpose.
~C. Avoid mentioning the main issue.#Incorrect. This weakens the purpose.
~D. Talk only about personal hobbies.#Incorrect. This distracts from the purpose.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which word is least academic for this presentation?{
~A. perspective#Incorrect. This is academic.
~B. coherent#Incorrect. This is academic.
~C. relevant#Incorrect. This is academic.
=D. stuff#Correct. Stuff is vague and informal.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which revision improves focus?{
=A. Remove the sentence about snacks and add a sentence explaining how quiet space supports homework completion.#Correct. This removes a distraction and adds relevant support.
~B. Add three more unrelated details.#Incorrect. That weakens focus.
~C. Remove the main claim.#Incorrect. The presentation needs a claim.
~D. Replace evidence with personal preference.#Incorrect. Evidence is stronger.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Which evidence would best support a claim about student safety?{
~A. The library has several book genres.#Incorrect. This does not address safety.
~B. Some students like quiet reading.#Incorrect. This is less directly about safety.
=C. Students waiting after dismissal need supervised locations.#Correct. This supports a safety claim.
~D. The cafeteria sells snacks.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which statement best describes a focused oral presentation?{
~A. It contains many unrelated facts.#Incorrect. That is unfocused.
~B. It hides the speaker's perspective.#Incorrect. The perspective should be clear.
~C. It changes purpose several times.#Incorrect. That confuses listeners.
=D. It uses connected details to support one clear perspective.#Correct. This describes coherent focus.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.R.07] If a speaker's purpose is to persuade, which detail is most useful?{
~A. A detail that distracts from the claim.#Incorrect. Distracting details weaken persuasion.
=B. A relevant reason showing why the audience should support the proposal.#Correct. Persuasion needs relevant reasons.
~C. A personal preference with no reason.#Incorrect. This is weak support.
~D. A random fact from another topic.#Incorrect. This does not support the claim.
}



::ENG2_U06_L03_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which final checklist best matches Lesson 3?{
=A. State a clear perspective, choose relevant details, remove distractions, and use academic vocabulary.#Correct. This is the Lesson 3 process.
~B. Mix unrelated details and avoid a claim.#Incorrect. This weakens focus.
~C. Include every note regardless of purpose.#Incorrect. Purpose should guide selection.
~D. Use only personal preference.#Incorrect. A strong presentation needs relevant support.
}
